1
00:00:01.333 --> 00:00:04.000
At this point, the purpose of the retriever is probably pretty clear.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:10.000
It needs to provide useful information to the LLM that was potentially not available when the model was trained.

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.000
Now let's take a look at how this component actually works.

4
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.000
Let's start with an analogy.

5
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Imagine you're visiting a library to answer the question,

6
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.000
How can I make New York style pizza at home?

7
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.000
I know, I know, you probably just looked this up online.

8
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.000
And honestly, so would I.

9
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.000
But stick with the metaphor because I think the visuals are helpful.

10
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.000
The library has a large collection of books on many topics.

11
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.000
To help you browse the collection, the books are organized in sections and shelves

12
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:47.000
based on characteristics of their topics, genre, authors, and so forth.

13
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:52.000
If you share your question with the librarian, they can help you find the sections of the library

14
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.000
or even the exact books that are most relevant to your question.

15
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Retrievers have many similar components.

16
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.000
Where a library has a collection of books, a retriever has a knowledge base of documents.

17
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.000
A retriever will similarly create an index of the documents in the knowledge base

18
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.000
which keeps the documents organized and makes them easy to search.

19
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:17.000
The next step is to actually retrieve relevant information.

20
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.000
In the library, you can just ask the librarian directly.

21
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.000
The librarian is able to understand the meaning of your question

22
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:30.000
and knows to go look in sections of the library on cooking or Italian cuisine or possibly New York.

23
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.000
The ability to interpret the meaning of your question

24
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.000
allows them to identify the right shelves of the library to search through

25
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.000
and eventually find relevant books.

26
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Inside a RAG system, the retriever does something similar.

27
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.000
It first needs to process the prompt to understand its underlying meaning.

28
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.000
It then uses that understanding to search the index of documents.

29
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:55.000
The retriever then returns the documents from the knowledge base

30
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.000
that it determines are the most relevant to the prompt.

31
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.000
When it's completing its search,

32
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.000
the retriever ranks documents in the knowledge base by how relevant they are to the prompt.

33
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:11.000
Each document receives a numerical score that quantifies its relevance.

34
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:17.000
Usually, this means some measure of the similarity between the text of the prompt

35
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:19.000
and the text of the document.

36
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.000
The documents with the highest scores are the ones that are returned.

37
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.000
There are a variety of approaches to calculating these similarity scores

38
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.000
that you'll learn more about later in the course.

39
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.000
A well-designed retriever should, of course, return relevant documents,

40
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.000
but it also needs to withhold irrelevant documents.

41
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.000
If you asked for information about making New York-style pizza at home

42
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.000
and the retriever responded with all the documents in the knowledge base,

43
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.000
technically, you'd have every relevant document,

44
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.000
but it'd be lost in a mountain of irrelevant information.

45
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.000
As you saw earlier, this would also lead to costly prompts

46
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.000
or even entirely use up the LLM's context window.

47
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.000
On the other hand, if you only retrieve the highest-ranked documents in the knowledge base,

48
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:11.000
you might miss valuable relevant information in the documents ranked 2nd, 3rd, or 4th.

49
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:17.000
In a perfect world, the retriever perfectly ranks the documents

50
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:21.000
and chooses the exact right number of them to return.

51
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:25.000
In practice, however, you know the retriever will sometimes rank

52
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:30.000
some relevant documents too low and rank some other irrelevant documents too high,

53
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.000
making it difficult to confidently decide how many of them to return.

54
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:39.000
To optimize a retriever's performance, you'll need to monitor it over time

55
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:44.000
and experiment with different settings, something you'll see extensively in this course.

56
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:50.000
It's worth noting that many familiar pieces of software perform very similar tasks to a retriever.

57
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:55.000
A web search engine retrieves web pages that are relevant to a web search,

58
00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:01.000
and a relational database retrieves rows and tables that match a SQL query.

59
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:05.000
The broader field of information retrieval was already mature

60
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:08.000
when large language models first were developed,

61
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:13.000
but ideas from this field underlie the way retrievers and RAG systems are designed.

62
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:17.000
In theory, there's lots of ways to implement the retriever in a RAG system.

63
00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:22.000
Since most companies already have their data in traditional relational databases,

64
00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:29.000
it'd be nice to keep the data there and figure out a way to retrieve from that database to power a RAG system.

65
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:36.000
While they're not strictly necessary, at scale, most retrievers will be built on top of a vector database.

66
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:42.000
A specialized type of database that is optimized for rapidly finding the documents in your knowledge base

67
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.000
that most closely match a prompt.

68
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:49.000
In this course, you'll learn about both general principles of information retrieval

69
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:54.000
that power a wide variety of search technologies and vector databases

70
00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:59.000
that are typically used as a retriever inside a production-scale RAG system.

71
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:04.000
There's lots more to learn about retrievers, but for now, that covers the most important points.

72
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:08.000
Join me in the next video, and let's wrap up this module.

